      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the net displacement of the traveler in both the north-south and east-west directions.\n\n1. **North-South Direction:**\n   - The traveler first heads 18 miles north.\n   - Then, the traveler heads 6 miles south.\n   - Net displacement in the north-south direction = 18 miles north - 6 miles south = 12 miles north.\n\n2. **East-West Direction:**\n   - The traveler first heads 11 miles west.\n   - Then, the traveler heads 6 miles east.\n   - Net displacement in the east-west direction = 11 miles west - 6 miles east = 5 miles west.\n\nNow, we need to determine the straight-line distance from the starting point to the final position of the traveler. This can be found using the Pythagorean theorem, which states that in a right-angled triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nLet \\( d \\) be the distance from the starting point to the final position. Then,\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{(12 \\text{ miles})^2 + (5 \\text{ miles})^2} \\]\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{144 + 25} \\]\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{169} \\]\n\\[ d = 13 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nTherefore, the traveler is \\(\\boxed{13}\\) miles from the starting point after these four legs of the journey.",
