      "text": "To determine the final position of the traveler, we need to calculate the net movement in the north-south direction and the net movement in the east-west direction. The traveler's net movement in the north-south direction is the difference between the northward and southward movements. The traveler's net movement in the east-west direction is the difference between the westward and eastward movements.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the net movement in the north-south direction:\nThe traveler initially moves 18 miles north and then 6 miles south. So, the net movement in the north-south direction is:\n\\[ 18 - 6 = 12 \\text{ miles north} \\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the net movement in the east-west direction:\nThe traveler initially moves 11 miles west and then 6 miles east. So, the net movement in the east-west direction is:\n\\[ 11 - 6 = 5 \\text{ miles west} \\]\n\nNow, we can determine the distance from the starting point using the Pythagorean theorem. The distance is the straight-line distance from the starting point to the final position, which is the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs of 12 miles (north) and 5 miles (west).\n\nThe distance \\(d\\) is given by:\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{12^2 + 5^2} = \\sqrt{144 + 25} = \\sqrt{169} = 13 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nTherefore, the traveler is \\(\\boxed{13}\\) miles from the starting point after these four legs of the journey.",
